      "latency_ms": 43080.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1078,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that for every molecule of water, there are two atoms of hydrogen and one atom of oxygen.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater molecules are made of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. The oxygen atom is larger and more electronegative than the hydrogen atoms, which causes the electrons in the bond to be pulled closer to the oxygen. This makes the oxygen end of the molecule slightly **negative** and the hydrogen end slightly **positive**. This property is called **polarity**, and it is the reason water is such a good solvent and why it sticks to itself.\n\n### 2. States of Matter\nWater is unique because it can exist naturally in all three states of matter at or near the surface of the Earth:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules move slowly and form a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid:** At room temperature, water flows and takes the shape of its container.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move very fast and spread out into the air.\n\n### 3. Unique Physical Properties\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is essential for transporting nutrients in the blood and plants.\n*   **High Specific Heat:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps the human body from overheating.\n*   **Density Anomaly:** Unlike most substances, water is less dense as a solid (ice) than as a liquid. This is why ice floats on water, which insulates lakes and oceans, allowing life to survive underneath the ice during winter.\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\nWater is the foundation of life on Earth. It is essential for:\n*   **Biological Functions:** It acts as a medium for chemical reactions in cells and helps regulate body temperature through sweating and panting.\n*   **Ecosystems:** It supports agriculture, provides habitats for aquatic life, and regulates the weather."
